Subject: [PATCH] arm64: do not treat cache maintenance faults as writes

A cache maintenance instruction run by user space (dc cvau, which a JIT uses to
make new code visible to the instruction cache) on a page it may read but not
write raises a fault with both the write bit and the cache-maintenance bit (CM)
set in ESR. This kernel took it for a write and killed the process with SIGSEGV.
Upstream fixed it in Linux 3.11, "arm64: Do not report cache maintenance faults
as writes" (db6f41063cbd); this is that commit's one line. ESR_EL1_CM was
already defined here.

Android's runtime knows of the bug: on any arm64 kernel older than 3.12 it
assumes the fault and does not create the JIT of the 64-bit zygote ("Zygote
memory only works with dual mappings"), so no 64-bit app, system_server or
SystemUI has a JIT and code not compiled ahead of time is only interpreted.
tools/tree-patches/art/0001 tells it that this kernel has the fix.
---
--- arch/arm64/mm/fault.c
+++ arch/arm64/mm/fault.c
@@ -223,7 +223,7 @@
 
 	if (esr & ESR_LNX_EXEC) {
 		vm_flags = VM_EXEC;
-	} else if (esr & ESR_EL1_WRITE) {
+	} else if ((esr & ESR_EL1_WRITE) && !(esr & ESR_EL1_CM)) {
 		vm_flags = VM_WRITE;
 		mm_flags |= FAULT_FLAG_WRITE;
 	}
